WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.780
Fast warp-specialized kernels split each thread block

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.860
into roles that run at the same time —

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.160
one loading, one multiplying, one writing results —

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.260
and hand off work through asynchronous copies and

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.280
barriers that count bytes.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.940
Such a kernel is a concurrent program,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.340
and it fails like one.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.680
A missing wait does not crash anything:

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.620
in some schedules, the output is silently wrong;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.480
in others, the same kernel computes the right result.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.740
A test sees only the schedules that happen to occur.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.860
SentryWard is a verifier for these kernels.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.540
Given a kernel's PTX, a launch configuration and a PyTorch reference,

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.640
it proves that the kernel's barriers behave the same way

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.500
in every schedule, that no schedule has a data race,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.060
and that the kernel computes the same function as the reference.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.650
The same analysis rules out deadlocks and uses of freed resources.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.810
When a check fails, SentryWard reports a witness.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:53.670
Six parts, one chain:

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:56.627
Part one specializes the kernel for its launch:

00:00:56.887 --> 00:00:59.287
branches resolved, loops unrolled,

00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:01.627
threads that behave alike grouped together.

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:03.707
Part two records the program twice:

00:01:03.947 --> 00:01:06.747
as events, for when things happen, and as values,

00:01:06.947 --> 00:01:07.767
for what they compute.

00:01:08.557 --> 00:01:11.097
Part three checks that every barrier behaves the same way

00:01:11.097 --> 00:01:12.337
in every schedule,

00:01:12.337 --> 00:01:14.757
and turns the synchronization into a happens-before graph.

00:01:15.557 --> 00:01:18.237
Part four requires every pair of conflicting accesses to

00:01:18.237 --> 00:01:19.497
be ordered by that graph.

00:01:19.677 --> 00:01:23.217
A missing order is a race, reported with its two accesses.

00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:27.322
Part five follows one serialization, which stands for all of them,

00:01:27.502 --> 00:01:28.462
and eliminates memory:

00:01:28.762 --> 00:01:31.122
each output becomes an expression of the inputs.

00:01:31.931 --> 00:01:34.911
Part six checks that expression against the PyTorch reference,

00:01:34.911 --> 00:01:35.571
part by part.

00:01:36.981 --> 00:01:39.221
Of 169 kernels in the benchmark,

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:42.901
SentryWard verified 53 and flagged 32.

00:01:43.221 --> 00:01:46.481
It reports the rest as inconclusive, never as a pass.

00:01:47.491 --> 00:01:49.131
It found issues in popular libraries:

00:01:49.311 --> 00:01:52.411
a buffer TileLang reuses while the tensor core is still reading it,

00:01:52.631 --> 00:01:56.011
a signal CUTLASS sends before the write it announces has landed,

00:01:56.331 --> 00:01:59.051
and a missing barrier in PyTorch, already fixed upstream.

00:02:01.341 --> 00:02:03.301
Verification can also make kernels faster.

00:02:03.681 --> 00:02:05.921
Kernels often synchronize more than they need to:

00:02:06.201 --> 00:02:09.261
remove one synchronization operation, verify again,

00:02:09.441 --> 00:02:12.321
and if the kernel still verifies, the operation can go.

00:02:13.041 --> 00:02:16.121
Across 44 kernels on a B200,

00:02:16.121 --> 00:02:19.701
more than 40% of the synchronization turned out to be unnecessary,

00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:22.501
and removing it cut kernel time by a median of 3–7.5%,

00:02:22.501 --> 00:02:25.021
depending on the input size.

00:02:25.881 --> 00:02:28.201
For kernel writers, evidence for every failed check.

00:02:28.361 --> 00:02:31.321
For kernel agents, a result a machine can act on.

00:02:31.541 --> 00:02:34.501
For library maintainers, synchronization that can go.

00:02:35.281 --> 00:02:35.781
SentryWard.

00:02:35.781 --> 00:02:39.401
Formal verification of warp-specialized GPU kernels.

